# Only One Place: `pages/_app.js`

In Next.js, you can import a global CSS file **only** inside the custom `_app.js` component.

This is to avoid unexpected style clashes across pages and ensure proper order.

Create a file `styles/globals.css` and import it in `pages/_app.js`.

```jsx
// pages/_app.js
import '../styles/globals.css';

export default function App({ Component, pageProps }) {
  return <Component {...pageProps} />;
}
```

Now every page has access to these styles.

slides.codeSnippet:
// pages/_app.js
import '../styles/globals.css';

export default function MyApp({ Component, pageProps }) {
  return <Component {...pageProps} />;
}

slides.content:
# Example `globals.css`

```css
html,
body {
  padding: 0;
  margin: 0;
  font-family: -apple-system, sans-serif;
}

a {
  color: blue;
  text-decoration: none;
}

* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
```

These rules apply to every element in your app. Use them for resets, base typography, and theming variables.

slides.codeSnippet:
/* styles/globals.css */
html,
body {
  padding: 0;
  margin: 0;
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, Segoe UI, Roboto, sans-serif;
}

a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
